<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Tufayl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1105–<a href="/years/1185">1185</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Abubacer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Tufayl was an Andalusian philosopher, physician and court official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Serving as physician and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</a>, he introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> to the court and encouraged him to write his commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. His philosophical novel Hayy ibn Yaqzan tells of a man who reaches knowledge of God through reason alone on a desert island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl.jpg" alt="Ibn Tufayl" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibn_%E1%B9%ACufail,_Sayr_mulhimah_min_al-Sharq_wa-al-Gharb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ibn Tufayl was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadix">Guadix</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, and was educated by the scholar Ibn Bajjah, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Avempace. His family belonged to the Arab Qays tribe. He worked as a secretary to several leaders: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> governor of Granada, and in 1154 the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, one of whom was Abu Saʿid ʿUthman, a son of ʿAbd al-Mu'min. He then entered the service of Abu Yaʿqub Yusuf, who was a prince at the time and became the second Almohad caliph. Ibn Tufayl served him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and physician.</p><p>In 1169 he recommended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a>, known as Averroes, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> as his own future successor. Ibn Rushd later recalled that Ibn Tufayl had told him of the caliph's complaint that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s expression, or his translators', was disjointed and obscure. Urging him to summarise and clarify the books, Ibn Tufayl said that only his great age and the cares of his office kept him from doing it himself. When he retired in 1182 Ibn Rushd succeeded him, and Ibn Tufayl died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1185. The astronomer Nur ed-Din al-Bitruji was among his disciples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ibn Tufayl's best-known work is Hayy ibn Yaqzan, which means Alive, son of Awake and is also known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Philosophus Autodidactus. It is a philosophical romance, inspired by Avicennism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, about a feral child raised by a gazelle on a desert island who discovers ultimate truth through reasoned inquiry. Hayy meets a castaway named Absal and comes into contact with civilisation and religion. He concludes that imagery and material goods in religion are necessary for the multitude to live decent lives, but are distractions from the truth for those whose reason recognises this. The names of the characters and the title come from tales by Ibn Sina. The work was written as a response to al-Ghazali's The Incoherence of the Philosophers.</p><p>He also wrote a long poem in the rajaz metre on medicine, describing how to diagnose illnesses and find their cures, and he was an early supporter of dissection and autopsy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ibn al-Nafis answered Hayy ibn Yaqzan in the 13th century with a work known in the West as Theologus Autodidactus. Edward Pococke the Younger published a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation in 1671, and Simon Ockley's English translation followed in 1708. The book became a best-seller in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> in the 17th and 18th centuries. It may have inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-defoe">Daniel Defoe</a>'s Robinson Crusoe, and it is thought to have influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>'s idea of the tabula rasa in An Essay Concerning Human Understanding of 1690.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibn Tufayl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abu Yaqub Yusuf and Yaqub al-Mansur patronised the philosophers <b>Ibn Tufayl</b> and Averroes (Ibn Rushd), making the court a centre of the highest medieval intellectual culture, although al-Mansur briefly disgraced Averroes in 1195 under pressure from conservative jurists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tíjola, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Marrakech, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.35,-2.44&amp;year=1157" aria-label="Tíjola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ibn Tufayl"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4834 4372h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4834 4372h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.35,-2.44&amp;year=1157">What was here in 1157? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Upon this the whole Body seem'd to him a very inconsiderable thing, and worth nothing in respect of that Being, he believed once inhabited, and now had left it.&#8221;</p><footer>Hayy ibn Yaqzan, section 22 &#183; tr. Simon Ockley, 1708 &#183; of his hero Hayy, the self-taught philosopher, at the death of the roe that raised him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16831/pg16831-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…like the Image of the Sun which appears in a well-polish'd Looking-glass, which is neither the Sun nor the Looking-glass, and yet not distinct from them.&#8221;</p><footer>Hayy ibn Yaqzan, section 90 &#183; tr. Simon Ockley, 1708 &#183; of what his hero Hayy beheld in contemplation &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16831/pg16831-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…that he was of a distinct Species from the rest of Animals, and that he was created for another end, and design'd for something greater than what they were capable of.&#8221;</p><footer>Hayy ibn Yaqzan, section 71 &#183; tr. Simon Ockley, 1708 &#183; of what his hero Hayy concluded about himself &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16831/pg16831-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our Ancestors, of Happy Memory, tell us, that there is an Island in the Indian Ocean, situate under the Equinoctial, where Men come into the world spontaneously without the help of Father and Mother.&#8221;</p><footer>Hayy ibn Yaqzan, the opening of the tale &#183; tr. Simon Ockley, 1708 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16831/pg16831-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And when he perceiv'd that his Island was encompass'd by the Sea, he thought that there was no other Land in the World but only that Island.&#8221;</p><footer>Hayy ibn Yaqzan &#183; tr. Simon Ockley, 1708 &#183; of his hero Hayy, growing up alone on the island &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16831/pg16831-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
